= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <partial fraction decomposition> is $(x-1)/[x(x+1)]=-1/x+2/(x+1)$. Since both endpoints are positive, integration gives
$$
\boxed{\int_a^b\frac{x-1}{x(x+1)}dx=\left[-\ln x+2\ln(x+1)\right]_a^b=\ln\frac{a(b+1)^2}{b(a+1)^2}.}
$$

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use $\tan^2x=\sec^2x-1$ and the derivative of the <tangent>:
$$
\boxed{\int\tan^2x\,dx=\tan x-x+C.}
$$
The antiderivative applies on each interval avoiding the poles of $\tan x$.

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For $1<x<e$, the <natural logarithm> satisfies $0<\ln x<1$. Squaring therefore decreases its value, while $\ln(x^2)=2\ln x$. Pointwise comparison gives $(\ln x)^2<\ln x<\ln(x^2)$, so
$$
\boxed{I_3<I_1<I_2.}
$$
As a check, <integration by parts> gives $I_1=1$, $I_2=2$ and $I_3=e-2$, consistent with this order.

\Image[paper-1-logarithm-comparison.png]
{title=Pointwise ordering of the three logarithmic integrands}
{description=Original plot on $[1,e]$. The natural logarithm rises from zero to one, so its square lies below it, and twice the logarithm lies above it. Integrating preserves the strict order.}

= 5
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <cumulative distribution function> must tend to one at infinity, so $\boxed{k=1}$. Differentiation gives the <probability density function> $f_X(x)=e^{-x}$ for $x>0$ and zero for $x<0$; there is no atom at zero. This is an <exponential distribution> of rate one. Its first two moments are $\int_0^\infty xe^{-x}dx=1$ and $\int_0^\infty x^2e^{-x}dx=2$, so
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[X]=1,\qquad\operatorname{Var}(X)=2-1^2=1.}
$$

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The continuous nonnegative part has total mass $\lim_{y\to\infty}(1-e^{-y})/2=1/2$. The remaining mass is at $-1$, giving $\boxed{m=1/2}$. This is a <mixed probability distribution>, specifically a <mixture of a point mass and an exponential distribution>; its atom must be included in normalization.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Include the negative atom as well as the continuous part in the <expected value>:
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[Y]=\frac12(-1)+\frac12\int_0^\infty ye^{-y}dy=-\frac12+\frac12=0.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The second moment of the <mixed probability distribution> is $\mathbb E[Y^2]=\tfrac12(-1)^2+\tfrac12\int_0^\infty y^2e^{-y}dy=1/2+1=3/2$. Since the mean is zero,
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Var}(Y)=\frac32.}
$$

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The conditioning event selects the exponential component: $P(Y\geq0)=1/2$ and $P(Y\geq1)=e^{-1}/2$. Thus <conditional probability> gives
$$
\boxed{P(Y\geq1\mid Y\geq0)=\frac{e^{-1}/2}{1/2}=e^{-1}.}
$$
The atom at $-1$ is excluded by the conditioning event.

= 6
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <determinant> is $k-6$, so for $k\ne6$ the <inverse matrix> is
$$
\boxed{A^{-1}=\frac1{k-6}\begin{pmatrix}k&-3&0\\-2&1&0\\-4k&12&k-6\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
The specified equations use $k=4$, with right-hand side $(2,3,1)^T$. Multiplying by $A^{-1}|_{k=4}$ gives
$$
\boxed{(x,y,z)=\left(\frac12,\frac12,-1\right).}
$$
In particular the parameter in the inverse formula must be set to four, not left arbitrary for this numerical system.

= ii
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $k=1$, the required right-hand matrix has twice the first row of $A$, the same second row, and twice the third row. Left multiplication scales rows, so
$$
\boxed{B=\operatorname{diag}(2,1,2).}
$$
Equivalently, right multiply the given equation $BA=C$ by $A^{-1}$ to obtain the same answer.

= iii
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

At $k=6$, the second row is twice the first. The independent equations are $x+3y=0$ and $4x+z=0$. Therefore the <null space> is
$$
\boxed{(x,y,z)=t(-3,1,12),\qquad t\in\mathbb R.}
$$
It has dimension one, consistent with matrix rank two.

= 7
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <trace> is unchanged by transposition and is cyclic under a product. With $S^T=S$ and $A^T=-A$,
$$
\operatorname{tr}(AS)=\operatorname{tr}[(AS)^T]=\operatorname{tr}(-SA)=-\operatorname{tr}(AS).
$$
Thus $\boxed{\operatorname{tr}(AS)=0}$. In components this is the vanishing contraction of a <symmetric tensor> with an <antisymmetric tensor>.

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <Levi-Civita symbol> vanishes when two indices coincide, so
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{tr}M=\delta_{ii}+2\epsilon_{iik}a_k=3.}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The antisymmetric contribution is the negative of twice the <cross-product matrix> for $\mathbf a$:
$$
M=\begin{pmatrix}1&2a_3&-2a_2\\-2a_3&1&2a_1\\2a_2&-2a_1&1\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The <cross-product matrix spectrum> is $0,\pm i|\mathbf a|$, so $M$ has eigenvalues $1,1\pm2i|\mathbf a|$. Multiplication gives
$$
\boxed{\det M=1+4|\mathbf a|^2.}
$$
Direct expansion of the displayed matrix yields the same real positive determinant.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Use index contraction:
$$
(MN)_{ij}=(\delta_{ik}+2\epsilon_{ik\ell}a_\ell)a_kb_j=a_ib_j+2\epsilon_{ik\ell}a_ka_\ell b_j=N_{ij}.
$$
The last term vanishes because $a_ka_\ell$ is symmetric in the contracted indices while the <Levi-Civita symbol> is antisymmetric. Hence $\boxed{MN=N}$.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Taking <determinants> in $MN=N$ gives $(\det M-1)\det N=0$. Here $\det M-1=4|\mathbf a|^2>0$ because $\mathbf a\ne0$, so $\boxed{\det N=0}$. This also agrees with $N=\mathbf a\mathbf b^T$ being an <outer product> of rank one.

= 8
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

On the straight path $\mathbf r(t)=(t,t,t)$, $0\leq t\leq1$, both fields restrict to $(3t^5,2t^5,t^5)$. Dotting with $\mathbf r'=(1,1,1)$ gives
$$
\boxed{\int\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r=\int\mathbf G\cdot d\mathbf r=\int_0^16t^5dt=1.}
$$

= ii
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $\mathbf r(t)=(t,t^2,t^2)$, the velocity is $(1,2t,2t)$. Because $y=z$ throughout this path, both fields again agree pointwise, now as $(3t^8,2t^7,t^7)$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\int\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r=\int\mathbf G\cdot d\mathbf r=\int_0^1(3t^8+4t^8+2t^8)dt=1.}
$$
These equal values alone do not establish <path independence> for either field on all of space.

= iii
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Direct differentiation gives $\boxed{\Phi=x^3y^2z+C}$ with $\nabla\Phi=\mathbf G$, so $\mathbf G$ is a <conservative vector field> and its integral depends only on the endpoints. In contrast,
$$
\nabla\times\mathbf F=\left(-2x^3y,\ 3x^2z(2y-z),\ 3x^2z(2y-z)\right)
$$
is nonzero, for example at $(1,1,1)$. Thus **$\mathbf F$ is not conservative**. Both earlier paths lie in the surface $y=z$, where the two fields happen to coincide. This illustrates why <coinciding path integrals need not imply a conservative field>.

