= Solution
{parent=i}

By <Euler's formula>, $\boxed{e^{2i}=\cos2+i\sin2}$, with the angle measured in radians.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Since the arguments of $-1$ are $(2k+1)\pi$, the <complex logarithm> has values $\boxed{\log(-1)=i(2k+1)\pi}$ for $k\in\mathbb Z$. The principal value is $\boxed{i\pi}$.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The modulus of $i$ is one and its arguments are $\pi/2+2k\pi$. Thus $\boxed{\log i=i(\pi/2+2k\pi)}$, with principal value $\boxed{i\pi/2}$.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For the positive real base ten, use its real <natural logarithm>. Then <Euler's formula> gives
$$
\boxed{10^i=e^{i\ln10}=\cos(\ln10)+i\sin(\ln10).}
$$

= v
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

Using a value of the <complex logarithm>, $i^i=\exp(i\log i)=\exp[-(\pi/2+2k\pi)]$. Thus every logarithmic-branch value is real and positive:
$$
\boxed{i^i=e^{-\pi/2-2k\pi}+0i,\quad k\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
The principal value is $\boxed{e^{-\pi/2}}$.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <hyperbolic cosine> and <hyperbolic sine> are defined by $\boxed{\cosh x=(e^x+e^{-x})/2,\quad\sinh x=(e^x-e^{-x})/2}$. Their quotient is $\tanh x=\sinh x/\cosh x$.

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Expand the two squares using the exponential definitions:
$$
\boxed{\cosh^2x-\sinh^2x=\frac{(e^x+e^{-x})^2-(e^x-e^{-x})^2}{4}=1.}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The exponential definitions show that $\sinh$ is <odd> and $\cosh$ is <even>. Their quotient therefore satisfies $\boxed{\tanh(-x)=-\tanh x}$.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Differentiating the exponential definition gives $\boxed{(\cosh x)'=(e^x-e^{-x})/2=\sinh x}$.

= c
{parent=9}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The fifth power of the <odd> function $\tanh x$ is odd and continuous. Its integral over the symmetric interval is therefore $\boxed{\int_{-1000}^{1000}\tanh^5x\,dx=0}$.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The derivative of $\ln\cosh x$ is $\tanh x$. Since $\cosh(\ln2)=(2+1/2)/2=5/4$,
$$
\boxed{\int_0^{\ln2}\tanh x\,dx=[\ln\cosh x]_0^{\ln2}=\ln\frac54.}
$$

= 10
{parent=Paper 1}
