= a
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Take the real part of <De Moivre's theorem>. Put $c=\cos\theta$ and use $\sin^2\theta=1-c^2$:
$$
\cos5\theta=c^5-10c^3(1-c^2)+5c(1-c^2)^2=\boxed{16\cos^5\theta-20\cos^3\theta+5\cos\theta}.
$$

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The modulus equation says that $z$ has fixed distance eight from $-1-i$. Thus its locus is **a circle of radius eight centered at $(-1,-1)$**, with Cartesian equation $(x+1)^2+(y+1)^2=64$.

\Image[paper-1-complex-loci.png]
{title=The original circle, a horizontal stretch, a translation and a quarter-turn}
{description=Original complex-plane loci. The map $u=2x+iy$ stretches only the horizontal coordinate; $v=z+4+3i$ translates the circle; $w=iv$ rotates that translated circle about the origin. Marked dots show each center.}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Writing $u=X+iY$ gives $X=2x$, $Y=y$. Substitute $x=X/2$, $y=Y$ into the original circle equation. This <affine image of a circle> is the <ellipse>
$$
\boxed{\frac{(X+2)^2}{16^2}+\frac{(Y+1)^2}{8^2}=1,}
$$
centered at $(-2,-1)$ with horizontal semiaxis sixteen and vertical semiaxis eight. The map covers the whole ellipse as $z$ covers the circle.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The complex translation adds four to the real coordinate and three to the imaginary coordinate. The locus is therefore **a circle centered at $(3,2)$ with radius eight**:
$$
\boxed{(X-3)^2+(Y-2)^2=64.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Multiplication by $i$ rotates the plane counterclockwise through $\pi/2$. The center $3+2i$ maps to $-2+3i$, while distances are preserved. Thus the locus is **a circle centered at $(-2,3)$ with radius eight**:
$$
\boxed{(X+2)^2+(Y-3)^2=64.}
$$

= c
{parent=10}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For $ab\ne0$, elimination of the trigonometric parameter gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{x^2}{a^2}+\frac{y^2}{b^2}=1.}
$$
As real $\theta$ varies, the whole <ellipse> is traced, with semiaxes $|a|,|b|$. Signs of $a,b$ affect the traversal direction but not the set. If $a=0$, the locus is the vertical segment $x=0$, $|y|\leq|b|$; if $b=0$, it is the horizontal segment $y=0$, $|x|\leq|a|$. Both zero give only the origin.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $ab\ne0$, the hyperbolic identity gives $x^2/a^2-y^2/b^2=1$. The <hyperbolic parametrization of a hyperbola> also imposes $x/a=\cosh\theta\geq1$, so the exact traced locus is
$$
\boxed{\frac{x^2}{a^2}-\frac{y^2}{b^2}=1,\qquad\frac xa\geq1.}
$$
It is only one branch: the right branch for $a>0$ and the left branch for $a<0$. Its vertex is $(a,0)$ and its asymptotes are $y=\pm(b/a)x$. If $a=0$, $b\ne0$, the entire vertical axis is traced. If $b=0$, $a\ne0$, the locus is the ray $y=0$, $x/a\geq1$. Both zero give the origin.

\Image[paper-1-ellipse-hyperbola.png]
{title=A trigonometric ellipse and the single hyperbolic branch traced by real parameters}
{description=Original example with $a=2,b=1$. The ellipse is closed, whereas $(a\cosh\theta,b\sinh\theta)$ traces only the right branch of the hyperbola. Dashed lines show its two asymptotes.}

= 11
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

First retain the <equilibrium> solution $y=0$. For $y\ne0$ and $x\ne\pm1$, <separation of variables> gives $\int y^{-2}dy=\int x/(1-x^2)dx$, hence $-1/y=-\tfrac12\ln|1-x^2|+C$. Renaming the constant yields
$$
\boxed{y=0,\qquad\text{or}\qquad y=\frac2{\ln|1-x^2|+C}.}
$$
A nonzero solution is considered on a regular interval avoiding $x=\pm1$ and zeros of its denominator. The zero solution itself extends through both singular points of the divided equation.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The initial value is positive, so set $z=y^{-2}$. The <chain rule> gives $z'=-2y^{-3}y'=-2(y^{-2}-1)$, making $z'+2z=2$. Its solution is $z=1+Ce^{-2x}$. Since $y(0)=1/2$, $z(0)=4$ and $C=3$. Choosing the positive branch gives
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac1{\sqrt{1+3e^{-2x}}},\qquad x\in\mathbb R.}
$$
It satisfies the initial condition and approaches the stable positive equilibrium one as $x\to\infty$.

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

On an interval with $\sin x\ne0$, divide by $\sin x$ and use <integrating factor> $1/\sin x$. Then
$$
\left(\frac y{\sin x}\right)'=\frac{\cos^3x}{\sin^2x}.
$$
Put $u=\sin x$ in the integral: $\int\cos^3x\,dx/\sin^2x=\int(u^{-2}-1)du=-u^{-1}-u+C$. Multiplying back gives
$$
\boxed{y(x)=-1-\sin^2x+C\sin x.}
$$
Although the derivation divided by $\sin x$, this final expression is smooth and directly satisfies the original undivided equation at every real $x$, including multiples of $\pi$.

= 12
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=12}

Use <cylindrical coordinates> with $dV=r\,dr\,d\theta\,dz$, $0\leq\theta<2\pi$, $0\leq z\leq h$ and $0\leq r\leq a(1-z/h)$. Set $s=1-z/h$. Integrating first over radius gives the mass per unit height
$$
\frac{dM}{dz}=2\pi\rho_0\left[\frac{a^2s^2}{2}+\frac{a^2s^3}{3}\right]=\pi\rho_0a^2\left(s^2+\frac23s^3\right).
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{M=\pi\rho_0a^2h\int_0^1\left(s^2+\frac23s^3\right)ds=\frac12\pi\rho_0a^2h.}
$$
For the first moment about the base, use $z=h(1-s)$:
$$
Md=\pi\rho_0a^2h^2\int_0^1(1-s)\left(s^2+\frac23s^3\right)ds=\frac7{60}\pi\rho_0a^2h^2.
$$
Dividing by the total mass gives
$$
\boxed{d=\frac{7h}{30}.}
$$
The <center of mass of a cone with a power-law radial density> gives an independent decomposition: the uniform-density component has centroid $h/4$, while the component proportional to $r$ has centroid $h/5$ and half as much mass. Their mass-weighted mean is $7h/30$. Density increasing toward the wider outer parts consequently moves the center below the uniform cone's $h/4$.
